{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Excel vs. Google Calendar vs. Gantt chart"}, {"type": "position", "content": 11}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to decide on project planning tools."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work to a using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Interpret the requirements report to determine the technological needs in managing information, based on rules and protocols established by the company."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Participates in simple conversations or simulated situations about the communicative topics for this level through grammar and vocabulary structures.  Recognizes simple ideas on requirement reports, tools for Object Oriented Programming, and other basic programming tools."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob11re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Be Going to in Affirmative to Talk about Plans"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>We use the <strong>&ldquo;be going to&rdquo;</strong> structure to talk about plans and possibilities.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>The structure of be going to always includes the verb <strong>&ldquo;be&rdquo; </strong>conjugated plus the going to and the action in infinitive after to.</p> <p>Examples:</p> <p><i>I <strong>am</strong><strong>going to</strong> design a Gantt chart for my math project.</i></p> <p>Remember that you can always use the contractions of verb <strong>&ldquo;be&rdquo;</strong> to simplify your statement.</p> <p>Examples:</p> <p><i>We&rsquo;<strong>re</strong> going to collect the requirements. </i></p> <p><i>I&rsquo;<strong>m</strong> going to start with the first task of my project.</i></p> <p>Differently to the present continuous for near future activities, going to can be used for short, mid, and long-term plans and possibilities.</p> <p>Examples:</p> <p><strong><i>In two years</i></strong><i> the company is going to implement a new software.</i></p> <p><strong><i>Next year</i></strong><i> we&rsquo;re going to finish the project.</i></p> <p><strong><i>Tomorrow </i></strong><i>I&rsquo;m going to visit the client&rsquo;s office. </i></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "The collaborative project we are going to design tomorrow."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'm going to check your deadlines and compare projects."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We are going to use Gantt charts in our forthcoming projects. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "nitty-gritty "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the essential or fundamental parts of something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: When it comes to the nitty-gritty details, we cannot differentiate sequence tasks from parallel."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob11aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "put one's hands on "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to start working on something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We just have to put our hands right on planning."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob11aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "bottleneck "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a stage where the processes slow down"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We cannot differentiate sequence tasks from parallel, nor visualize bottlenecks along the progress of a project."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob11aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tiresome "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "annoying and tiring"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: That will be tiresome."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob11aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "unlike "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "differently "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(preposition)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Unlike Excel, Google calendar is a collaborative tool."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob11aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "milestone "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a significant or important event or stage"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You create a project with project duties and milestones."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob11aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Choose one of the chart systems we studied"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create your own calendar and program your activities for the next two weeks."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Present your calendar to the class and explain how did you organize it."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I use chat tools to create a calendar"}, {"type": "text", "content": "I organize information in sequence "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I explain how to design a calendar using chat tools."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Five Tools to Create Online Charts"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Think about this: You have a presentation, and there is a lot of information to show. What can you use? Online tools may help you to create amazing charts."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>You can use some online charts creators with different tools and options for you to present data in an original, smart way.</p> <p>There are many features you can add to your charts in order to make them look better and dynamic.</p> <p>Have a look at the features bellow and choose the one that best fits your data and style.</p>"}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob11img01.jpg"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I heard that Gantt charts were the best tool for project planning."}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n3le01ob11re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob11re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<p><strong>David</strong>: Good morning, Sarah, I need some advice on the best chart to create the project calendar for the collaborative project we are going to design tomorrow.</p>\n<p><strong>Sarah</strong>: I always use Excel. It&rsquo;s a great tool to design calendars, and it has pre-made calendar templates, so we don&rsquo;t have to design anything. We just have to put our hands right on planning.</p>"}, {"time": "19500", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob11re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p><strong>David</strong>: Excel calendars are good, but there&rsquo;s a disadvantage; Excel is an off-line tool, and it doesn&rsquo;t permit us to edit the template simultaneously. I don&rsquo;t want to consolidate all the versions at the end of the project. That will be tiresome. Excel is the least efficient chart.</p>\n<p><strong>Sarah</strong>: What about Google calendar? Unlike Excel, Google calendar is a collaborative tool; it has lots of advantages. We work on the calendar in real time from different locations because it&rsquo;s cloud-based, and we can design the project either by the assignee, you create a calendar per person and add the task milestones in our personal calendar; or by project, you create a project with project duties and milestones.&nbsp;</p>"}, {"time": "61000", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob11re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p><strong>David</strong>: Yes, I&rsquo;m going to check your deadlines and compare projects, Google calendar is good, but when it comes to the nitty-gritty details, we cannot differentiate sequence tasks from parallel, nor visualize bottlenecks along the progress of a project.&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Sarah</strong>: I heard that Gantt charts were the best tool for project planning. You usually spread all the tasks and data on a timeline, so you can have a holistic view of the project. You can also put sequence tasks together to organize the project, and see more than one project on the same chart.</p>\n<p><strong>David</strong>: That sounds great. We are going to use Gantt charts in our forthcoming projects.</p>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n3le01ob11re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can compare different types of charts."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can talk about advantages and disadvantages among different types of charts."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can give details about different types of charts."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What tools do you use to organize your projects?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What are the advantages and disadvantages of designing calendars?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], 